Not a chance, for reasons already stated.

> They're the hardest class in the game.

Not really; they're pretty easy in the early game if
you use pet management skills to prevent early
deaths, and play to use their strengths rather than
to be subject to their weaknesses.

They _can_ be played as protection racketeers, but
that's pretty hard to do compared to, say, wizards,
so it's better to take alternate paths.

They are self-repairing, trading PW for HP to
recover HP much faster than other characters, by
casting healing on ".".

They start with at least 25 HPs after quaffing all
their initial potions.

They are nearly starvation-proof to an attentive
player, once they reach the needed PW to cast stone
to flesh.

They start with a huge cash stake and very high
charisma, making credit cloning a snap and so the
protection racket much less necessary.

All that early and earnable cash makes _purchasing_
AC and powerful weaponry and protection a readily
available option.

Running away works exceptionally well for lightly
burdened healers, so long as the starting pet
survives. If the healer starts with at least 5 PW
(it can instead be as low as 3 PW, often), following
the pet with stethescope and doing pet healing casts
helps the pet survive and buff up rapidly. An early
leash or magic whistle can be strong survival
promoters.

I cannot really see the Healer, with all these
advantages, as particularly difficult. The big
visible disadvantages healers have are
straightforward to overcome, poor starting weapon,
poor starting armor, and poor starting strength.

The less visible one is lack of enhancability of
big swords, but crowning eventually lets the long
sword be enhanced.

Visiting Mines fixes the armor and weapon problems,
pushing boulders and slamming doors obsessively
fixes the strength problems. These can be done
simultaneously with buffing up the starting pet.

Stacking up some quivered daggers, and weilding a
dagger in melee, improves dagger skills fairly
quickly to skilled, plenty good enough for a
consensual coward play-style.

Thus with care, and as my current PC shows, healers
are fairly easy to bring to par with other PC types.

My huge problem isn't the choice of PC type, it is
simply that I can't keep my mind focused on the game
long enough to take advantage of this set of
characteristics, playing in far too much haste,
always.

